{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR RULING (recorded verbatim on the operator's behalf, before design work): \"An operation-linked GC trigger is a reasonable approach, BUT we have encountered issues with long locks blocking the ticket store on local operations — the design must avoid that pitfall, likely using the same pattern we used to fix that issue.\"\n\nThe prior art the ruling points at: closed bug 0d15-59a4-* (\"Sweep walks full git history per ticket under the store lock\") and the compaction trigger's fix pattern in src/rebar/_commands/compact_trigger.py — a near-free in-process check on the tail of the operation, then the heavy work in a DETACHED child outside the calling operation and outside any store lock, with stamp/lock sidecars keyed on the canonical store (dae7f62696) and a durable child cwd (_proc.detached_child_cwd, d575600381), plus the O(1) marker fast-path discipline from a02eedbb6e. The design for this ticket must mirror that pattern: the trigger check itself must hold NO ticket-store lock and must not scale with store size; the GC pass runs detached under the snapshot store's own gc flock."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787335368477430001,"uuid":"cf04ab1b-64b7-43b1-83d7-80cc6262f7c5"}